{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Root-cause note (regression origin):\n\nThe J11 store-copy fixture (dc_store_copy_repo in tests/external/live_jira_dc/_dc_fixtures.py) scrubs .bridge_state*, commits the scrubbed tree, then calls run_ensures() to converge the git-archive copy into a writable store (needed for the .env-id store marker).\n\nrun_ensures runs the 'projects-seed' ensure unit (rebar._store.project_ensures.seed_projects_mapping_unit), added by commit 72731d3745 on 2026-08-13 (ticket 462d / epic 0303). That unit's tree-check is 'git show tickets:.bridge_state/projects.json'; when it misses — exactly the state the scrub leaves — it re-creates AND commits .bridge_state/projects.json. So convergence silently resurrects the very cache the scrub removed, and the isolation cell then sees .bridge_state survive.\n\nThe scrub itself (2026-08-07, epic e369) predates that seed unit, and before epic 0303 run_ensures created nothing under .bridge_state, so the scrub held. This is a pure ordering regression: a new unconditional ensure unit re-introduced an artifact after the one-shot scrub.\n\nFix: extract scrub_bridge_state(tracker, *, commit=False); re-run it (with commit=True) AFTER run_ensures so the resurrected .bridge_state/projects.json is removed from worktree and tickets tree. Principled (re-scrub the whole .bridge_state* glob) rather than special-casing one filename; keeps .env-id intact. Pinned by a fast repo-tier regression test that drives the real seed unit as a negative control."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786728067904079001,"uuid":"5e5664d0-fa6e-4ada-a514-0092f16d0fb0"}